# Lapis Cloud -- PRODUCTION coturn config. This is a TEMPLATE, not the real file: the `${...}`
# placeholders below are substituted from deploy/production/.env by render-secrets.sh (see
# README.adoc "Video conferencing"). The rendered turnserver.conf is gitignored -- never commit it.
#
# `realm` below is substituted from `LAPIS_TURN_REALM` in deploy/production/.env by
# render-secrets.sh, same as every other `${...}` value in this file -- it used to be a
# hand-edited-in-place prose placeholder (`realm=PROD_HOST`) directly in this TRACKED template,
# which meant an operator who committed their edits after a deploy (`git add -A && git commit`)
# would push the real realm hostname straight into this public repo. Moved to an env var (round 6
# of "Redact real infra topology from public repo") so the real value lives only in the gitignored
# `.env`, never in a tracked file.
#
# Mirrors deploy/local/turnserver.conf (see that file's own header for why `use-auth-secret`/
# `static-auth-secret` is used instead of a static long-term username/credential pair -- coturn's
# REST-API shared-secret scheme, same as dev: this value is never itself sent to a client,
# `TurnCredentialMinter.kt` derives a fresh short-lived credential per `joinRoom` call from it). The
# load-bearing PRODUCTION difference is host networking (see below), not an address setting.
listening-port=3478
min-port=51000
max-port=51099
# 100 relay ports (was 20): every relay client holds several ports for as long as an allocation lives; with 8 relay clients and constant table
# changes 20 ports ran out (staging load test, 2026-10-19: 758 'no available ports' lines, 25 % of the relay clients' connects timed out).
realm=${LAPIS_TURN_REALM}
use-auth-secret
static-auth-secret=${LAPIS_TURN_SECRET}
# NO `external-ip`, on purpose (2026-10-18). This coturn runs in HOST networking (docker-compose.yml): it
# binds the host's real addresses, so it needs no address mapping. With `external-ip` and the coturn on
# a Docker bridge next to LiveKit on the same host, relayed media from LiveKit never reached the client
# (measured on staging: a client forced onto the TURN relay never connected, with the coturn in host
# networking the same run had no error). See README.adoc "TURN relay: host networking and the TTL rule".
# A TURN client must not use the relay to reach the host's own networks (host networking, see above): deny private,
# link-local and loopback peers and multicast, and allow only this host's public address (LiveKit is reached through it).
# For a LAN-only test setup whose own address is private, remove the denied-peer-ip lines.
no-multicast-peers
denied-peer-ip=0.0.0.0-0.255.255.255
denied-peer-ip=10.0.0.0-10.255.255.255
denied-peer-ip=100.64.0.0-100.127.255.255
denied-peer-ip=127.0.0.0-127.255.255.255
denied-peer-ip=169.254.0.0-169.254.255.255
denied-peer-ip=172.16.0.0-172.31.255.255
denied-peer-ip=192.168.0.0-192.168.255.255
allowed-peer-ip=${LAPIS_PUBLIC_IP}
no-dtls
fingerprint
log-file=stdout
no-cli

# ── TURNS over TLS (port 443) -- ACTIVE since 2026-09-12 ────────────────────────────────────────
# Why: a browser with a restrictive WebRTC privacy policy (Brave's Shields / fingerprinting
# protection) or a network that only permits outbound 443 can block BOTH the direct ICE path AND
# plain TURN on 3478/UDP+TCP. Symptom: audio/video never start even though the camera/microphone
# permission was granted. Reported live by an ELB board member, 2026-09-10. See
# ../production/README.adoc, "TURNS over TLS (port 443)".
#
# Rollout (2026-09-12): a DNS A record (turn.PROD_HOST -> this host's IP), a real
# Let's Encrypt certificate obtained by Caddy itself (see the Caddyfile's two cert-only vhosts),
# and `sniproxy` doing SNI-based TCP multiplexing on the host's public 443 -- Caddy no longer binds
# 443 directly (moved to :8443, see README.adoc), coturn's TLS listener stays on an INTERNAL port,
# never on 443 directly. `cert`/`pkey` (NOT `cert-file`/`pkey-file` -- that was this wave's own
# first, wrong guess, coturn logs "Bad configuration format" and silently refuses to start the TLS
# listener with the wrong key names) point at the files `/usr/local/sbin/sync-turn-certs.sh` (run
# by the `sync-turn-certs.timer` systemd timer, daily) copies out of Caddy's own certificate store
# whenever Caddy renews them.
tls-listening-port=5350
cert=/etc/coturn/certs/turn-fullchain.pem
pkey=/etc/coturn/certs/turn-privkey.pem
